{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "player absence",
    "offensive reorganization",
    "initiation profile similarity",
    "teammate gainer",
    "primary play types",
    "pre-absence profile matching",
    "substitution effect",
    "minutes as proxy",
    "initiations as proxy",
    "same-position minutes as proxy"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure closest pre-absence initiation profile (similarity metric, feature set, time window)?",
    "How to define 'largest gainer' in the absent player's primary types (absolute vs. relative change, volume vs. efficiency)?",
    "How to determine the absent player's primary play types (top N, threshold)?",
    "How to handle absences when multiple players are missing simultaneously?",
    "How to control for opponent strength, home/away, and back-to-back games?",
    "How to deal with regression to the mean when selecting spells based on extreme pre-absence usage?",
    "What is the statistical power with only 11 cases (or 148 spells) and many candidate teammates per spell?",
    "How to handle exposure sparsity for rarely used play types?",
    "How to define pre-absence period (fixed games, full season prior, rolling window)?",
    "What multiplicity correction is needed when testing multiple proxies (similarity, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes)?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Similarity of pre-absence initiation profiles does not separate absorption from cascade: mean similarity 0.863 for absorbed vs 0.848 for cascade; Spearman correlation between similarity and absorption gap is -0.19 (wrong direction). Embiid 2023-24 absence cascaded to Paul Reed despite a similarity of 0.94."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
      "what_failed": "Using pre-absence initiation-profile similarity to predict whether the most similar teammate absorbs the absent player's role (absorption vs. cascade)",
      "why": "Adequately powered null result (failure mechanism B_powered_null). Similarity was nearly identical between absorbed and cascade cases and the correlation with the absorption gap was weakly negative. Risk of label/classification error (RISK:LABEL_ERROR) may also have contributed."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Pre-absence initiation-profile similarity does not reliably identify the teammate who takes over the absent player's role; the correlation with uptake is near zero or weakly negative."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether the closest-profile teammate is the largest gainer specifically in the absent player's primary play types (the prior study used a binary absorption/cascade outcome, not rank of gain within primary types).",
    "Whether alternative proxies (most minutes, most initiations, same-position minutes) predict the largest gainer.",
    "Whether the failed similarity result generalizes to larger samples with different choice of primary types or similarity measure.",
    "The effect of other confounders (concurrent injuries, opponent strength, game context) on gainer identification."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "With only 11 distinct absence cases (or 148 spells) the study would be severely underpowered to detect any effect of similarity or other proxies on the rank of gainer among teammates, given the expected small effect sizes.",
    "The prior failure of similarity to predict even the binary absorption outcome strongly suggests that a more granular rank-based outcome will also be null unless the alternative proxies have much larger effects, which is speculative.",
    "Defining 'primary types' and 'largest gainer' will introduce further classification noise (e.g., rare play types have very few pre-absence events), increasing the risk of label error and low statistical power.",
    "Even if minutes or same-position serving as the best predictor could be plausible, the tiny n makes it unlikely that any effect can be reliably separated from noise."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "What exactly constitutes the 11 'cases' vs 148 'spells' (e.g., 11 players with multiple absence spells each) and whether the unit of analysis is the spell or the player.",
    "The operational definition of 'primary types' (e.g., top-1, top-2, or types exceeding a usage threshold) and how many such types exist per absent player.",
    "The number of eligible teammates per spell and the variance of their gains.",
    "Whether the available per-game play-type classification is the derived '55-series' or a different taxonomy, and its stability for low-minute players."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The historical evidence from FAM:SF-CHT-052 demonstrates that pre-absence initiation-profile similarity does not separate absorption from cascade, with an adequately powered null result. This directly undermines the core hypothesis of the current question and indicates that even if refined as a rank-based outcome among primary types, similarity is unlikely to succeed. The finding shifts the prior probability of a positive result dramatically downward and reinforces the decision not to proceed."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.03,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 11 cases (or 148 spells, if cases are distinct players) with multiple teammates per spell; power to detect a small effect of any single proxy is likely below 20% even under optimistic assumptions."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Primary play types of the absent player may have very low pre-absence volume for some players, making pre-absence initiation profiles for those types noisy and unreliable."
      },
      {
        "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
        "text": "Defining 'largest gainer' and 'primary types' is subjective; small differences in definition can flip which teammate is labeled the gainer, especially when multiple teammates' gains are close."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Other player absences during the same spell, opponent strength, game location, and rest days can affect which teammate's usage increases, confounding the relationship with similarity/minutes."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF-CHT-052"],
    "summary": "The project previously tested whether a similar-profile teammate absorbs the absent player's role (absorption vs. cascade) and found an adequately powered null result (inconclusive). The current question extends this to rank of gainer within primary types and adds alternative proxies, but the core idea that profile similarity predicts who takes over has already been refuted."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Extremely small number of independent absence cases (11) or spells (148) yields low power; even a moderately large effect may not be detectable."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Pre-absence initiation volumes for some play types are low, making similarity computations unreliable and causing unstable rankings of teammates."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Operationalization of 'primary types' and 'largest gainer' involves arbitrary thresholds; the outcome may be sensitive to noise and misclassification."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Unmeasured confounders such as concurrent injuries, defensive gameplans, and game script are likely to influence which teammate's usage increases, masking any true proxy effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "If absence spells are selected from periods when the absent player had high usage of certain play types, regression to the mean can distort both the pre-absence similarity and the post-absence gain measure."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing four candidate proxies (similarity, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) without correction inflates the false positive rate."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.01,
    "rejected": 0.69,
    "untestable": 0.2,
    "artifact": 0.05,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "For each absence spell, the effect of being the teammate with the highest pre-absence initiation-profile similarity (or most minutes, most initiations, or most same-position minutes) on being the teammate with the largest absolute increase in usage (possessions) of the absent player's primary play types, compared to the other teammates on the floor.",
    "unit": "Absence spell (a continuous absence of one player, defined as consecutive missed games with the same player out) or, alternatively, the within-spell teammate-level observation.",
    "sample": "All absence spells from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular season and playoffs where the absent player missed at least one game, with sufficient pre-absence data (≥10 team games played in the preceding two months) to estimate initiation profiles and primary play types. Exclude spells with multiple core rotational players missing simultaneously. The available data contains 148 spells (11 cases); power analysis suggests at least 200 independent spells to detect a moderate rank correlation (τ ≈ 0.2) with 80% power at α = 0.05.",
    "power": "Negligible with the current n=11 cases / 148 spells; power would remain low even for a strong effect (e.g., τ=0.3, n=148 gives power ≈ 50% for a one-sample Spearman test under optimistic assumptions). A naive split-half validation would be impossible.",
    "controls": "Game-level fixed effects: home/away, days since last game, opponent defensive rating (season to date), number of other missing rotation players. Spell-level: number of games in the spell, whether it includes back-to-backs.",
    "falsification": "1) Shuffle similarity (or proxy) ranks across spells while preserving the gain values; the observed rank correlation should fall inside the null distribution. 2) Use placebo absences (games where the player played but we artificially treat them as absent) to check that no spurious rank correlation emerges.",
    "multiplicity": "If testing all four proxies simultaneously, apply Bonferroni correction (α/4) or use a hierarchical testing procedure that first requires the overall likelihood-ratio test to be significant before examining individual proxies.",
    "decision_rule": "For each spell, compute a within-spell Spearman rank correlation between the proxy rank (1=highest similarity/minutes/etc.) and the gain rank (1=largest increase in primary-type possessions). Test whether the mean correlation across spells differs from zero using a one-sample t-test (or permutation test). Declare supported if the corrected p < 0.05 and the mean correlation is positive and practically meaningful (≥0.15).",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 per-game play-by-play derived play-type possession records as of 2026-11-01, with defensive matchup minutes and substitution intervals."
  }
}}

